\section{Related Works}

\paragraph{Accelerating dLLM inference.} Unlike autoregressive models, dLLMs promise to decode multiple tokens in parallel. However, overly aggressive parallel decoding severely degrades generation quality. 
To unleash the advantages of dLLMs, much research has been devoted to accelerating their inference process, ranging from designing better training-free decoding strategies with theoretical guarantees, such as Fast-dLLM~\citep{wu2025fastdllmtrainingfreeaccelerationdiffusion}, EB-Sampler~\citep{ben-hamuAcceleratedSamplingMasked2025}, KLASS~\citep{kim2026klass}, and Prophet~\citep{li2025diffusion}, to directly learning optimal decoding strategies~\citep{jazbec2025learning,chen2026dparallel,chen2025dultra}.
These methods accelerate decoding but do not explicitly train the model on early states to bridge the early-vs-final gap.

\paragraph{Baselines.} 
We compare our method against two primary baselines. 
First, we evaluate the respective base models without further training, employing the same \texttt{Fast-dLLM} decoding procedure. 
To distinguish between the initialization sources across tasks, we denote these baselines as \texttt{LLaDA (Fast-dLLM)} and \texttt{GDSD (Fast-dLLM)}. 
Second, we compare against \texttt{dParallel}~\citep{chen2026dparallel}, an externally confidence-distilled checkpoint. 
Because dParallel checkpoints are unavailable for Countdown and Sudoku, we omit this baseline for those two tasks.


\subsection{Training Datasets and Details}

\paragraph{Training Datasets.}
For GSM8K, we use the training split of the GSM8K dataset~\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/openai/gsm8k}}. 
For MATH-500, we train on the training split of the MATH dataset~\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/EleutherAI/hendrycks_math}}. 
For Countdown, we train on the training split of the dataset~\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/Jiayi-Pan/Countdown-Tasks-3to4}} from the TinyZero project~\citep{tinyzero}. 
For Sudoku, we use the training dataset provided by Black-Phoenix~\footnote{\url{https://github.com/Black-Phoenix/4x4-Sudoku-Dataset}}, which is also available locally in the \textit{d1} codebase. 
For coding tasks, we train on the KodCode-Light-RL-10k dataset~\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/KodCode/KodCode-Light-RL-10K}}. 

\paragraph{Training Details and Hyperparameters.}
All experiments are conducted on 2 NVIDIA A100/H200 GPUs.
Each training run lasts for approximately 3,000 steps. We use checkpoint 2000 for reporting the final results across all tasks, except for MATH-500, where we use checkpoint 1000.
We fine-tune with LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} using AdamW~\citep{loshchilov2018decoupled}. 
Each training step draws $4$ rollouts per prompt (\texttt{num\_generations}$=4$). 
Rollouts are generated with \texttt{Fast-dLLM} block-parallel decoding (block length $32$) at a fixed training-time
confidence threshold of $0.9$.
