\paragraph{LoRA configuration.}
We fine-tune \texttt{LLaDA-8B-Instruct} ($8.02\text{B}$ parameters) with
LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} at rank $r=64$, scaling $\alpha=32$, and dropout $0.05$
(no bias, RSLoRA disabled). Because the number of adapted weights---and hence the effective capacity of the update---is determined by \emph{which} modules are targeted rather than by the rank alone, we report the target set explicitly: LoRA adapters are inserted into the query, key, value, and MLP up projections (\texttt{q\_proj}, \texttt{k\_proj}, \texttt{v\_proj}, \texttt{up\_proj}) of all $32$ transformer blocks, while all other weights---including the attention output and remaining feed-forward projections, the embeddings, and the layer norms---are kept frozen at their pretrained values. This yields $N_{\text{trainable}} = 83.9\text{M}$ trainable parameters ($1.05\%$ of the base model). 

\paragraph{Countdown training canvas.}
For Countdown, we train TSD with a response canvas of $L=128$, unlike the other benchmarks which use $L=256$. When training Countdown at $L=256$, we obtain strong performance when evaluating at $L=256$ and $L=512$, but performance at $L=128$ degrades noticeably. We therefore train at $L=128$, which recovers the short-canvas performance while preserving strong results when evaluated on larger canvases. Thus, using the shorter training canvas provides better robustness across $L \in \{128, 256, 512\}$. The cross-canvas evaluation results for the model trained at $L=256$ are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:countdown_L256train}.

